"""Version 1: a more direct, restrained authorial voice. Hand-written paragraphs."""
EDITS = {
0: r"""A science map is useful only if its conclusions can be interpreted with some confidence in how the map was made. Here, ten embedding models represent the same fixed corpus of 500,000 OpenAlex records from 26 fields and 2000--2024. Matched panels compare field centres, internal structure and nearest neighbours, then vary text input and representation settings. Broad structure is shared, but models retain only 30.3\% of the 25 nearest neighbours on average within field-period groups. Removing abstracts changes neighbours about as much as changing the model; removing titles changes less. An exploratory comparison of all 325 field pairs finds persistent opposite orderings across models in 262 pairs for angular spread and 221 for effective linear dimension. Requiring relative differences above 5\% leaves 96 and 177. Alternative measures preserve many oppositions, while centring and pooling affect their persistence. These are geometric findings, without an external test of thematic correctness. A comparison can survive repeated article selection and still reverse when the model changes. Robustness assessment should follow the particular conclusion drawn from a science map, alongside its overall agreement with other maps.""",
1: r"""A science map helps a reader decide which papers to place together and how to compare research areas. With text embeddings, that organisation starts when a model turns each paper into a vector. Distances between vectors then provide the relationships on which the map rests. This approach has been used to map biomedical literature and investigate the disciplinary structure of physics \citep{refbe130e2973,refc0e2cc29e0}. Its usefulness leaves a practical question: if another model represented the same papers, which conclusions would still hold?""",
2: r"""Science mapping has faced this problem before. \citet{refa0d2d707e6} asked how far a reconstructed thematic structure reflects scientific knowledge or the method used to recover it. \citet{ref6a2f5912bc} compared text, citation and hybrid relations and found substantial changes in cluster membership despite similar aggregate accuracy scores. A close overall score can therefore conceal a difference that matters to a reader: the methods may group different papers together.""",
3: r"""This comparison keeps the articles fixed and follows the consequences of changing their representation. The corpus contains 500,000 publications labelled English in OpenAlex, published in 2000--2024 and assigned to 26 broad Fields. Ten embedding models represent those papers. The analysis moves from relations between field centres to the structure within fields and the neighbours of individual articles. Matched subsets vary the input text and the rule used to reduce model output to one vector per paper. A later geometric extension tests whether a comparison between two fields keeps its direction when the model and article selection change.""",
4: r"""The models share broad relationships, yet differ in local neighbours and in comparisons between fields. A geometric ordering can recur across article selections in one model while the opposite ordering recurs in another. Its magnitude and sensitivity to processing determine how much to make of that disagreement. This places a limit on what repeated selection alone can establish. Agreement between representations identifies shared structure; assessing whether that structure captures the subject matter requires further evidence.""",
5: r"""Several studies already show why a document representation needs an explicit purpose. \citet{refc0e2cc29e0} assessed text and graph representations against a hierarchical classification of physics papers, including representations of titles and abstracts. In biomedicine, \citet{refbe130e2973} compared models and output-reduction rules before choosing a representation for a large literature map. Their criterion was how often nearby papers shared field labels. In both cases, the interpretation of performance depends on the criterion used to judge it.""",
6: r"""Naming the model leaves several choices unspecified. A title and an abstract supply different information; models also accept different amounts of text. Some require a further decision about how to combine token representations into a document vector. This operation, called pooling, can average the token outputs or use one designated position. \citet{singh-singh-2022-inefficiency} showed that scientific language models can change their retrieval behaviour under short queries and changes in wording. A comparison of science maps should make these input and representation choices visible.""",
7: r"""Geometry and retrieved neighbours have also been compared together. \citet{ref4e1e0d5582} used both across retrieval datasets, while \citet{ref6da178065c} examined local density and asymmetry across document embeddings and disciplines in relation to citation rates. The question pursued here is how far broad, local and geometric conclusions persist across a fixed model panel. Matching articles, text conditions, representation settings and candidate papers makes it possible to specify which change each comparison addresses.""",
8: r"""Agreement describes what two models share when they represent the same articles. Stability asks whether a stated result survives a stated change, such as selecting other articles from the corpus. These can come apart: a field ordering may be stable within each model and differ between models. Thematic validity asks a further question about whether the represented relationships describe the research appropriately. In invasion biology, \citet{QSSreviewQ17} showed why interpreting algorithmic groups requires attention to the different relationships a mapping method combines. Repetition and consensus alone cannot settle that interpretation.""",
9: r"""Here, \emph{shape} refers to relationships in the original embedding spaces, before any projection into two dimensions. Field centres describe broad organisation. Comparisons within fields retain article-level structure, and neighbour agreement identifies whether the same papers stay closest to a given article. Angular spread and effective linear dimension describe further geometric properties: how widely the vectors open out and how their variation occupies different directions. Their names need to stay close to those operations. Greater spread does not establish greater topic diversity, and effective linear dimension does not count topics. A change in one property also need not imply a change in every other part of the map.""",
11: r"""The questions describe the selected corpus and model panel. The third arose after the initial comparisons, in an exploratory extension whose sequence is recorded in the methods and supplement. OpenAlex labels organise the analyses, with their known errors retained. They provide no independent test of thematic correctness. The objective is to establish which conclusions persist within this design; recovering or ranking models against a single correct map would require a different form of evidence.""",
14: r"""The panels in Table~\ref{tab:T01} keep distinct comparisons apart. For the three text inputs, 52,000 articles are divided equally across Field-period groups, including the earlier pilot of 26,000. A different panel of 52,000 articles compares each model's native input with the same literal fragment, chosen to fit every tokenizer. Equal-size group comparisons and matched neighbour searches address further sources of variation. Article identity is held constant between models in every comparison. Supplement S1 gives the extraction rules, cleaning steps, counts and retained flags.""",
18: r"""A group centre averages its unit-length article vectors. Comparing centres therefore describes the organisation of groups, whereas comparing their constituent articles preserves variation within them. The random-group control asks how much centre agreement can arise from averaging without the observed labels. It keeps group sizes and publication periods fixed and uses the same random allocation in every model.""",
23: r"""The repeated selections test sensitivity inside the frozen corpus. Because the subsets overlap, their ranges cannot be read as confidence intervals from independent population samples. The operational screens flag excessive median shifts or selection ranges; the supplement keeps each rule and denominator separate. Model pairs share models, and Field pairs share Fields. Their counts are consequently descriptive, and no significance test treats them as independent observations.""",
24: r"""The work developed through successive comparisons. Shape and neighbours came first, followed by the expanded input panel, matched Subfield searches and case atlas. The morphology extension was exploratory; its all-pairs summary followed inspection of the initial examples. Rules were recorded locally before each corresponding new calculation, without externally preregistering the entire study. Random-group centres and the fixed-neighbour-fraction check were among controls added after earlier results. Supplement S2 records this sequence. Supplements S3--S7 retain the checks on time, discipline composition, model traits, source errors and processing, including those that restrict the interpretation.""",
26: r"""Inside the groups, agreement has a different level. Mean CKA is around 0.63 for Fields and 0.64 for the same 183 Subfields eligible at each size (Figure~\ref{fig:scales}B). Increasing the article count from 128 to 512 changes these averages little. Finer labels therefore do not imply lower agreement in this comparison. The centre and within-group panels describe different objects, so the gap between them cannot isolate a causal effect of scale.""",
30: r"""Shape and neighbour agreement describe related changes. Their descriptive rank correlation across the original 45 model-pair means is 0.920. There is still a practical reason to report both: CKA summarises distributed relationships, while a neighbour list identifies the articles a reader would actually encounter. The two measures expose different consequences of representation choice without being unrelated dimensions of reliability.""",
34: r"""The pooling rule can alter the comparison used to assess input changes. With mean pooling as the principal readout, removing the title has a relatively modest effect, and removing the abstract slightly exceeds model change in average neighbour replacement. Using CLS or SEP for the four word-level BERTs reverses that latter ordering. Four representations change together, including the reference for the cross-model comparison. This prevents an isolated causal reading of pooling and gives no basis for choosing the readout with the highest agreement.""",
36: r"""The geometric extension follows an individual claim about the map: whether one Field has a higher measured property than another. The first check changes the articles while retaining the model. Angular spread has no alerts among the 260 model--Field cases in either the half-sample or equal-size screen. Effective linear dimension has four half-sample alerts and none across the five equal-size selections (Table S12). The latter result does not remove the four retained alerts.""",
42: r"""The connection measures did not support the proposed interpretation as fragmentation. Under some rules, elongated clouds and isolated extreme points produced a similar signal, and changing the sample size or neighbour count substantially changed the result. The equal-size selection screen retained 51 connection alerts among 260 cases. Supplement S6 reports these measures together with the failed construct checks. They cannot support a claim that one Field contains more separate thematic groups.""",
43: r"""The broad structure shared by the models is a useful starting point for interpretation. Centre agreement exceeds the random-group reference, so the common organisation cannot be reduced to that reference alone. The Field-pair results show where the inference needs to go further. One model can repeatedly place two Fields in one order, and another repeatedly place them in the opposite order, as articles change. The relevant robustness test therefore concerns the relationship, neighbour set or Field comparison that the analysis intends to interpret.""",
44: r"""This question continues the comparative problem described by \citet{refa0d2d707e6,ref6a2f5912bc}. An external classification, as used by \citet{refc0e2cc29e0}, tests how a representation serves a particular criterion. Joint comparisons of alignment and retrieval, as in \citet{ref4e1e0d5582}, establish which relationships models share. The contribution here connects those levels to the persistence and magnitude of particular Field comparisons on matched articles. Earlier work already examined representation choice and supplied the agreement measures on which this comparison builds.""",
45: r"""Angular spread and effective linear dimension remain useful when interpreted as the geometric operations they measure. Angles describe separation; PR describes the allocation of variance across linear directions. Reading either as interdisciplinarity or semantic diversity requires evidence about the articles' content. The same care is needed when interpreting embedding isotropy \citep{rudman-etal-2022-isoscore}. The failed fragmentation checks make the issue concrete: a measure can reliably distinguish vector clouds without identifying the substantive property attached to its name.""",
46: r"""A useful way to assess robustness is to start with the claim a map will support. For retrieval, that requires stating the candidate pool and neighbour count. For a geometric comparison between Fields, it requires the representation rule, article balance and minimum difference. The input experiment adds a straightforward reporting obligation. A model name alone leaves the reader unable to reconstruct what was represented; the title/abstract choice, token limit and pooling rule belong beside it.""",
47: r"""The additional comparisons also place limits on explanations of disagreement. Medicine has lower shape agreement than Energy, but is not consistently lowest for neighbours. The Medicine--Energy difference remains after balancing Subfield composition and after excluding BioBERT and PubMedBERT (Supplement S5). Neither check supplies a unique cause. Model families also look different under different readouts. Architecture, training material and objectives vary together in this panel, preventing their separate causal attribution.""",
48: r"""The time comparison shows why the opportunity to find a neighbour matters. Mean shape agreement rises slightly from the first period to the last. Mean neighbour agreement falls by 0.87 percentage points with all candidates, but rises by 0.91 points when each group has 2,048 candidates. The direction changes with the search design. Current model parameters represent publications from different dates; they do not reconstruct the knowledge available at those dates. These comparisons cannot establish a historical convergence of science itself.""",
49: r"""The corpus bounds the interpretation. It contains selected OpenAlex records with usable abstracts and predominantly English text. The reinforcement sample, balanced panels and equal-weight averages support comparisons between Fields, without estimating the worldwide distribution of research. The atlas shows that classification and content errors remain. Cleaning removes specified failures; it cannot certify every label. Recorded selections and frozen checkpoints permit inspection of the comparison, while repeated selection within the corpus leaves its coverage bias in place.""",
50: r"""The model panel imposes a similar boundary. Its members share architectures, training sources and objectives, and several were not trained for document similarity. Checks using six similarity-oriented models and alternative readouts address particular concerns, without making the panel representative of all encoders. Persistent opposition identifies dependence on the models tested. It leaves open which ordering, if either, better describes the subject matter.""",
51: r"""The geometric extension and all-pairs summary also followed earlier results. Their recorded sequence and complete tables make the choices inspectable, while preserving their exploratory status. A useful next study would select one consequential comparison and test it against independently assessed article relationships or a question grounded in a discipline. That would supply evidence about thematic interpretation, which further agreement calculations alone cannot provide.""",
52: r"""The models share a broad organisation of the literature while differing in article neighbours and the geometric ordering of Fields. Input choice can matter as much as model choice. A comparison can also survive repeated article selection and still reverse between encoders. These results favour checking the particular claim a science map is used to support, alongside overall similarity. Angular spread and effective linear dimension allow such checks when definitions, magnitudes and processing sensitivities remain explicit. They describe the representations produced here. Further validation is needed before those geometric differences can be interpreted as properties of scientific knowledge.""",
}
